\section*{Gaussian propeller partition bound}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 096; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that every finite measurable Gaussian partition has squared first-moment sum at most $9/(8\pi)$, attained by three equal planar sectors with orthogonal extensions.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 096; 2026-09-24), optimization over partitions reduces to a finite family of Gaussian linear scores. Deleting a score and competing against pairs impose covariance constraints; certified scalar estimates eliminate five or more active cells and bound the remaining configurations sharply.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 096; 2026-09-24).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 096; 2026-09-24), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: every finite measurable Gaussian partition has squared first-moment sum at most $9/(8\pi)$, attained by three equal planar sectors with orthogonal extensions.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{GaussianPropeller.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.GaussianPropeller.all\_partitions}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

